    },
    {
      q: "How does Neptoon handle taxes on private pensions?",
      a: "Private pensions are treated separately. The model can recognize an existing deferred withdrawal-tax obligation and a separate tax on subsequent investment gains. Pension investment returns are assumed to be after ongoing pension investment tax, so that tax is not deducted again."
    },
    {
      q: "Why can selling an asset later change my Financial Freedom age?",
      a: "Selling an asset may trigger payment of deferred taxes. Postponing the sale also postpones that payment. Because the tax liability and your invested wealth do not necessarily grow at the same rate, the timing can affect your future net wealth."
    },
    {
      q: "Does Neptoon calculate my actual tax bill?",
      a: "No. Neptoon uses simplified tax assumptions to illustrate their economic impact over your lifetime. It does not calculate your personal tax return or reproduce every national tax rule, allowance or exemption."
    }
  ]
},



/* E — REAL ESTATE */
{
  id: "realEstate",
  letter: "E",
  title: "Real Estate",
  items: [
    {
      q: "Should I include the value of my home?",
      a: "Yes, if you want your home to be part of your total economic wealth. Enter its estimated market value today, not just your home equity. Any mortgage is entered separately under your loans."
    },
    {
      q: "What happens when I sell my home?",
      a: "Neptoon transfers the estimated sale value into your Free Portfolio, after deducting any outstanding mortgage and applicable deferred capital gains tax. Your property is then removed from the Balance Sheet and replaced by its net sale proceeds."
    },
    {
      q: "What if I never plan to sell my home?",
      a: "Leave the liquidation age blank. Neptoon then assumes the property is realized at the end of your expected lifetime, with its remaining net value contributing to your Legacy."
    },
    {
      q: "Does paying down my mortgage increase my wealth?",
      a: "Mortgage repayments reduce your outstanding debt, but the payments are also financed by your Free Portfolio. Repaying principal therefore does not create new wealth by itself. Interest payments are a financing cost that reduces your economic wealth."
    }
  ]
},

